{"module":"culture","voice":"male","duration":184.6,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Sports, music and culture","start":0.0,"end":2.83,"words":[[0.04,0.67],[1.04,1.67],[1.67,1.99],[1.99,2.62]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Sports and music are where many Americans first meet Black excellence, and where the color line was most visible, challenged and crossed.","start":3.63,"end":12.86,"words":[[3.67,4.07],[4.07,4.31],[4.31,4.79],[4.79,4.87],[4.87,5.11],[5.11,5.43],[5.43,6.16],[6.16,6.48],[6.48,6.72],[6.72,7.04],[7.04,7.76],[8.04,8.28],[8.28,8.52],[8.52,8.68],[8.68,9.0],[9.0,9.32],[9.32,9.56],[9.56,9.96],[9.96,10.6],[10.88,11.6],[11.6,11.84],[11.84,12.32]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Jack Johnson","start":13.21,"end":14.29,"words":[[13.25,13.56],[13.56,14.09]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Jack Johnson became the first Black world heavyweight boxing champion in 1908.","start":14.99,"end":20.58,"words":[[15.02,15.32],[15.32,15.84],[15.84,16.29],[16.29,16.44],[16.44,16.74],[16.74,17.04],[17.04,17.34],[17.34,17.93],[17.93,18.38],[18.38,18.98],[18.98,19.13],[19.13,20.1]]},{"el":"s0","text":"When he beat the white former champion James Jeffries in 1910, white mobs attacked Black Americans in cities across the country.","start":20.93,"end":28.8,"words":[[20.97,21.18],[21.18,21.32],[21.32,21.54],[21.54,21.68],[21.68,21.96],[21.96,22.25],[22.25,22.82],[22.82,23.25],[23.25,23.75],[23.75,23.89],[23.89,24.67],[24.92,25.21],[25.21,25.49],[25.49,25.85],[25.85,26.13],[26.13,26.78],[26.78,26.92],[26.92,27.28],[27.28,27.63],[27.63,27.77],[27.77,28.27]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In 1913 he was convicted under the Mann Act, widely seen as a racially motivated prosecution, and he was pardoned posthumously in 2018.","start":29.15,"end":38.16,"words":[[29.2,29.33],[29.33,30.09],[30.09,30.21],[30.21,30.4],[30.4,30.98],[30.98,31.23],[31.23,31.36],[31.36,31.55],[31.55,31.8],[32.02,32.4],[32.4,32.59],[32.59,32.72],[32.72,32.85],[32.85,33.29],[33.29,33.99],[33.99,34.75],[34.97,35.17],[35.17,35.29],[35.29,35.48],[35.48,35.86],[35.86,36.69],[36.69,36.82],[36.82,37.64]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Breaking the color line","start":39.06,"end":40.61,"words":[[39.11,39.66],[39.66,39.82],[39.82,40.13],[40.13,40.44]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Jesse Owens won four gold medals at the 1936 Berlin Olympics.","start":41.31,"end":46.46,"words":[[41.36,41.74],[41.74,42.12],[42.12,42.35],[42.35,42.5],[42.5,42.88],[42.88,43.34],[43.34,43.49],[43.49,43.65],[43.65,44.79],[44.79,45.17],[45.17,45.85]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Jackie Robinson broke Major League Baseball's color line in 1947, enduring threats and abuse.","start":46.81,"end":53.66,"words":[[46.86,47.21],[47.21,47.77],[47.77,48.13],[48.13,48.55],[48.55,48.76],[48.76,49.33],[49.33,49.61],[49.61,49.89],[49.89,50.03],[50.03,51.23],[51.48,52.11],[52.11,52.46],[52.46,52.68],[52.68,53.1]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Before him, Black players starred in the Negro Leagues; in 2020 MLB recognized those leagues as major leagues, and in 2024 it added their statistics to the official records, making Josh Gibson the career batting-average leader.","start":54.01,"end":68.89,"words":[[54.05,54.34],[54.34,54.63],[54.89,55.18],[55.18,55.62],[55.62,55.91],[55.91,56.05],[56.05,56.2],[56.2,56.64],[56.64,57.0],[57.26,57.41],[57.41,58.28],[58.28,58.72],[58.72,59.45],[59.45,59.74],[59.74,60.03],[60.03,60.18],[60.18,60.62],[60.62,60.98],[61.24,61.46],[61.46,61.6],[61.6,62.63],[62.63,62.77],[62.77,63.06],[63.06,63.28],[63.28,64.01],[64.01,64.16],[64.16,64.31],[64.31,64.74],[64.74,65.25],[65.51,65.95],[65.95,66.24],[66.24,66.68],[66.68,66.82],[66.82,67.19],[67.19,67.99],[67.99,68.36]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Athletes who protested","start":69.79,"end":71.54,"words":[[69.84,70.39],[70.39,70.58],[70.58,71.41]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Muhammad Ali refused to be drafted in 1967, was stripped of his title and convicted, and won his case at the Supreme Court in 1971.","start":72.24,"end":83.04,"words":[[72.29,72.82],[72.82,73.05],[73.05,73.58],[73.58,73.73],[73.73,73.88],[73.88,74.41],[74.41,74.56],[74.56,76.0],[76.26,76.49],[76.49,76.94],[76.94,77.09],[77.09,77.32],[77.32,77.77],[77.77,78.0],[78.0,78.75],[79.02,79.24],[79.24,79.47],[79.47,79.7],[79.7,80.0],[80.0,80.15],[80.15,80.3],[80.3,80.75],[80.75,80.98],[80.98,81.13],[81.13,82.49]]},{"el":"s2","text":"At the 1968 Olympics, Tommie Smith and John Carlos raised gloved fists on the medal stand; Australian silver medalist Peter Norman wore a human rights badge in solidarity.","start":83.39,"end":95.62,"words":[[83.44,83.59],[83.59,83.75],[83.75,85.04],[85.04,85.72],[85.99,86.3],[86.3,86.6],[86.6,86.83],[86.83,87.13],[87.13,87.59],[87.59,87.97],[87.97,88.35],[88.35,88.73],[88.73,88.88],[88.88,89.04],[89.04,89.42],[89.42,89.87],[90.14,90.9],[90.9,91.28],[91.28,91.89],[91.89,92.2],[92.2,92.58],[92.58,92.73],[92.73,92.88],[92.88,93.34],[93.34,93.72],[93.72,94.02],[94.02,94.18],[94.18,95.01]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In 2016 quarterback Colin Kaepernick knelt during the national anthem to protest police violence; he has not played in the NFL since 2017 and settled a grievance with the league in 2019.","start":95.97,"end":108.56,"words":[[96.02,96.16],[96.16,97.07],[97.07,97.63],[97.63,97.98],[97.98,98.48],[98.48,98.76],[98.76,99.25],[99.25,99.39],[99.39,99.88],[99.88,100.23],[100.23,100.37],[100.37,100.93],[100.93,101.29],[101.29,101.92],[102.16,102.31],[102.31,102.52],[102.52,102.73],[102.73,103.08],[103.08,103.22],[103.22,103.36],[103.36,103.78],[103.78,104.06],[104.06,105.05],[105.05,105.26],[105.26,105.68],[105.68,105.82],[105.82,106.31],[106.31,106.52],[106.52,106.66],[106.66,106.87],[106.87,107.01],[107.01,108.0]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In 2020, WNBA players helped elect a challenger to their own team's co-owner after she criticized the league's racial justice stance.","start":108.91,"end":117.98,"words":[[108.96,109.11],[109.11,110.03],[110.28,111.28],[111.28,111.71],[111.71,112.06],[112.06,112.42],[112.42,112.56],[112.56,113.2],[113.2,113.34],[113.34,113.56],[113.56,113.77],[113.77,114.06],[114.06,114.56],[114.56,114.84],[114.84,114.98],[114.98,115.7],[115.7,115.84],[115.84,116.12],[116.12,116.55],[116.55,117.05],[117.05,117.48]]},{"el":"h3","text":"The Black roots of American music","start":118.88,"end":121.69,"words":[[118.93,119.13],[119.13,119.52],[119.52,119.92],[119.92,120.12],[120.12,120.91],[120.91,121.5]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Spirituals, the blues, jazz, gospel, rhythm and blues, rock and roll (Sister Rosetta Tharpe was an early pioneer), funk and hip-hop, born at a party in the Bronx in 1973, all came out of Black communities.","start":122.39,"end":138.63,"words":[[122.45,123.42],[123.7,123.86],[123.86,124.26],[124.54,124.95],[125.23,125.79],[126.07,126.48],[126.48,126.72],[126.72,127.12],[127.4,127.64],[127.64,127.89],[127.89,128.21],[128.21,128.61],[128.61,129.17],[129.17,129.42],[129.42,129.66],[129.66,129.82],[129.82,130.06],[130.06,130.7],[130.99,131.31],[131.31,131.55],[131.55,132.11],[132.39,132.64],[132.64,132.8],[132.8,132.96],[132.96,133.28],[133.28,133.44],[133.44,133.6],[133.6,134.09],[134.09,134.25],[134.25,135.7],[135.98,136.14],[136.14,136.46],[136.46,136.7],[136.7,136.86],[136.86,137.19],[137.19,138.07]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Billie Holiday's 1939 recording of “Strange Fruit” brought the horror of lynching to a national audience.","start":138.98,"end":146.11,"words":[[139.02,139.33],[139.33,139.94],[139.94,141.09],[141.09,141.62],[141.62,141.78],[141.78,142.39],[142.39,142.69],[142.69,143.0],[143.0,143.15],[143.15,143.46],[143.46,143.61],[143.61,144.15],[144.15,144.3],[144.3,144.45],[144.45,144.99],[144.99,145.52]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Many Black musicians of the early record industry were paid flat fees and lost their royalties and rights.","start":146.46,"end":153.13,"words":[[146.51,146.82],[146.82,147.14],[147.14,147.84],[147.84,148.0],[148.0,148.15],[148.15,148.39],[148.39,148.78],[148.78,149.41],[149.41,149.56],[149.56,149.88],[149.88,150.19],[150.19,150.42],[150.42,150.66],[150.66,150.97],[150.97,151.21],[151.21,151.83],[151.83,152.07],[152.07,152.54]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Appreciation or appropriation?","start":154.03,"end":156.58,"words":[[154.08,154.92],[154.92,154.99],[154.99,156.04]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Big Mama Thornton's 1953 recording of “Hound Dog” was a hit on the rhythm-and-blues charts; Elvis Presley's 1956 version sold millions and made him a star, while she earned little.","start":157.28,"end":170.46,"words":[[157.33,157.57],[157.57,157.88],[157.88,158.43],[158.43,159.61],[159.61,160.17],[160.17,160.32],[160.32,160.72],[160.72,160.95],[160.95,161.19],[161.19,161.35],[161.35,161.58],[161.58,161.74],[161.74,161.9],[161.9,162.84],[162.84,163.32],[163.59,163.99],[163.99,164.54],[164.54,165.8],[165.8,166.19],[166.19,166.59],[166.59,167.14],[167.14,167.37],[167.37,167.69],[167.69,167.93],[167.93,168.08],[168.08,168.4],[168.67,168.99],[168.99,169.15],[169.15,169.38],[169.38,169.86]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Some see stories like this as theft that left Black artists poor while others profited; others see cultural exchange that spread Black art.","start":170.81,"end":179.48,"words":[[170.86,171.09],[171.09,171.24],[171.24,171.71],[171.71,172.02],[172.02,172.25],[172.25,172.41],[172.41,172.72],[172.72,172.95],[172.95,173.26],[173.26,173.57],[173.57,174.04],[174.04,174.27],[174.27,174.58],[174.58,174.89],[174.89,175.59],[175.86,176.17],[176.17,176.33],[176.33,177.03],[177.03,177.8],[177.8,178.04],[178.04,178.43],[178.43,178.74],[178.74,178.97]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Most agree that credit and pay should follow the art.","start":179.83,"end":183.72,"words":[[179.88,180.31],[180.31,180.66],[180.66,180.92],[180.92,181.44],[181.44,181.71],[181.71,181.97],[181.97,182.23],[182.23,182.66],[182.66,182.84],[182.84,183.1]]}]}